.TH CONTROLTEST 1
.SH NAME
controltest \- check control(2) against the textbook answers
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B controltest
[
.B -v
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Controltest
checks
.IR control (2)
and prints
.B OK
if everything agrees.
.B -v
prints each measured number.
.PP
A second-order system's step response is checked against
.RI 1\- e \u\s-2\-\fIzeta\fP\fIwn\fP\fIt\fP\s+2\d(cos
.IR wd t
+
.IR zeta wn / wd
sin
.IR wd t )
at every sample, and its overshoot against
.RI exp(\-pi* zeta /sqrt(1\- zeta \u\s-22\s+2\d)),
which is the first formula in the subject.
A polynomial built from known roots must give those roots back.
An integrator is exactly 1/\fIw\fP with exactly \-90 degrees of phase.
The loop
.IR k /(\fIs\fP(\fIs\fP+1)(\fIs\fP+2))
is stable up to exactly
.I k
= 6 and oscillates at exactly sqrt(2) radians per second, which is also
the gain margin of the same loop at
.I k
= 1.
.SS The check that is not against a formula
.B Routh
and
.B stable
answer the same question by completely different means \- one from the
coefficients, one by finding every root \- and they are run against each
other on three thousand random polynomials of degree 2 to 6.
Neither is the oracle; agreeing is the evidence.
The count of stable and unstable cases is checked as well, so that a run
in which everything happened to be stable cannot pass for agreement.
.PP
Five polynomials with roots
.I on
the imaginary axis are checked separately, since the random sweep skips
them: they are neither stable nor unstable, and they are exactly what
makes a whole row of the Routh array zero.
That row must be replaced by the derivative of the polynomial the row
above stands for.
The dodge used for a single zero in the first column \- substituting a
small number \- gives the
.I wrong
count here, and that is how it is known the derivative branch is doing
the work: without it those five answer 3, 2, 3 and 3 instead of 1, 0, 1
and 1.
.SS Two checks that exist because of a blindness
Every system in a controls course is strictly proper, and so was every
system this program tested.
A system whose numerator has the
.I same
degree as its denominator has a direct path from input to output, which
the state-space form must subtract out of the output matrix \- and in a
strictly proper system that correction is multiplied by zero and cannot
be seen.
Deleting it passed every check here.
.RI ( s +1)/( s +2)
has the step response 1/2 + exp(\-2\fIt\fP)/2, which starts at 1 and not
at 0, and it is now checked.
.PP
The same system is what
.B impulseresponse
must refuse, since its impulse response contains a delta.
.SS The same RC a third way
.IR Circuit (2)
integrates the network,
.IR signal (2)
filters the samples, and
.IR control (2)
integrates the transfer function.
Three independent pieces of code for one first-order lag, each compared
with 1\-exp(\-\fIt\fP/\fIRC\fP) rather than with each other, since each
has its own step size and its own error.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/controltest.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR control (2),
.IR circuit (2),
.IR signaltest (1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Each disagreement prints a line beginning
.B FAIL
naming what was measured, what came out and what was wanted; the count
follows and the program raises an exception.
.SH BUGS
The random polynomials are drawn with coefficients between \-5 and 5,
which produces far more unstable cases than stable ones \- about fifteen
to one.
Both are required to appear, but the stable side is tested much less
heavily than the count suggests.
.PP
Nothing here checks
.B margins
against a system with two gain crossings, which is the case its fixed
frequency grid handles worst.
.PP
The accuracy of the Runge-Kutta integration is checked at one step size
per system.
A first-order bug in something billed as fourth-order would pass if its
error at that step were small enough.
